Signup / free-trial abuse

Status: not implemented. Identity is Clerk; the gateway never sees a signup.
Request rate limits and the credits ledger both key by user id, so N fake
Clerk accounts are N independent free allowances. That is the actual attack.
Tightening `/chat/stream` does not touch it.

Why this exists

The free tier is 1,000 credits/month plus storage. Credits and GCRA bound one
account. Farmers mint accounts. The unit of abuse is therefore a cheap
identity, not a chatty session.

What already covers a *different* attack (leave it alone):

- Cloudflare IP rate limit on `api.abcd.com` (volumetric floods). Do not enable
  Bot Fight Mode — it cannot skip `/webhooks/` and will challenge Stripe/Clerk
  deliveries. See `openwiki/deployment-runbook.md` §2.
- Go GCRA: per-user AI / upload / general classes, plus 3 concurrent chat
  streams. Fail-open if Redis is down.
- Credits reserve/settle, keyed by actor.
- Upload class exists because ingest GPU is spent *after* the HTTP call
  returns; the worker itself is not rate-limited.

Do not invent a homemade “confidence factor” (email age, social graph, device
hash → abuse probability) on day one. Cursor turned a similar scorer up against
trial fraud and locked out paying users. Use vendor tools (Clerk, Cloudflare,
Stripe Radar) until farming is observed and support can unwind false positives.


What to do, in order of leverage
--------------------------------

1. Make identity expensive *before* handing out credits

   Best: card-on-file even if never charged (Stripe Radar). The card is the
   expensive identity; Radar clusters stolen/prepaid/reused instruments.

   Next: Clerk phone verification. Blocks throwaway email. VoIP and virtual
   numbers get filtered; real SIMs are still farmed. Conversion cost is real —
   do not turn this on before farming is visible unless the free grant is large.

   Also: Clerk “block disposable email domains”. Cheap, do it now. OAuth-only
   (Google/GitHub) helps a little; those accounts are farmed too.

2. Challenge bots on the *signup* path, not the API

   Cloudflare Turnstile / Clerk Bot Protection on Clerk-hosted sign-up. Not
   Bot Fight Mode, and not a WAF challenge on `/webhooks/` or `/api/`.

3. Velocity at identity, not at chat

   N signups per IP / ASN / fingerprint / phone per day. This lives in Clerk +
   Cloudflare. The gateway cannot see signups today (`clerkWebhook` only
   provisions a `users` row after the fact).

   After signup, bind the *free grant* to a stable signal: same card, same
   phone, same device cluster → one allowance, not N. That is the real
   confidence factor. Stripe Radar + Clerk fraud features are that product;
   do not store a DIY score in `users`.

4. Keep request limits as a backstop, then loosen them

   Once credits gate spend, the AI hourly cap can be looser (the billing
   revamp’s 200/hr + short 15/min window). Keep upload tighter than chat —
   one request is minutes of L4, billed after the fact. Keep 3 concurrent
   streams. Do not flatten AI / upload / general into one bucket; request
   cost still varies by orders of magnitude.

5. Device fingerprint (web analog of Cursor’s machine id)

   Cursor’s “too many free trials on this machine” is desktop-only and
   spoofed constantly; it still raises cost. For this app the analog is
   Cloudflare bot score + Clerk device, not a first-party fingerprint.js
   pipeline. Cookie / localStorage checks are worthless against farmers.


When to reach for the next rung
-------------------------------

- Disposable-email block + Turnstile on sign-up: now, low conversion cost.
- Loosen AI request limits: with the credits/registry revamp, not before.
- Phone or card-on-file: when you see clusters of new users burning the free
  grant in the first session, or when the free allowance grows.
- Custom clustering / support-driven bans: only after ops can see signup
  velocity next to spend (the operator dashboard). False positives need a
  human path; Cursor’s automated “create a new account” reply made this worse.


